<h1 class="article-title">Development of Liepāja Industrial Park Continues with Site Remediation</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority continues transforming the former Liepājas metalurgs steelworks site by implementing the project “Development of the Liepāja Industrial Park Territory, Phase 2”. The project involves rehabilitating a historically contaminated industrial area and building modern infrastructure needed for business. It is being implemented under the European Union Cohesion Policy Programme for 2021–2027, supporting the transition to a climate-neutral economy and mitigating the related socioeconomic impacts in the Kurzeme region.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis explains that the aim is to transform the former Liepājas metalurgs plant into a modern industrial park, creating space for new business initiatives. Businesses in Liepāja are growing and therefore need new sites for manufacturing and logistics projects. The industrial park will provide room for expansion and attract new investment to Liepāja. Hmieļevskis is particularly pleased that locally owned businesses see the park as a suitable location for their factories.</p><p>The project will dismantle nine heavy fuel oil tanks with a combined capacity of 60,000 cubic metres and remediate soil and groundwater in the area where Eksporta Street will be built. Modern utilities, including district heating, water supply, wastewater and stormwater networks, will be installed, and Eksporta and Čuguna streets will be connected into a single road network.</p><p>Construction is being carried out by A-Land SIA, under a contract signed on 28 August 2026 worth EUR 3.88 million excluding VAT. According to A-Land construction manager Dainis Kopštāls, the project presents a challenge because the site must be remediated before utilities and a section of road can be built. “Good planning by the client and the professionalism of our partners allow us to proceed on schedule.” Construction supervision is provided by Firma L4 SIA, design supervision by BM – Projekts SIA, and environmental consulting by Ameco SIA.</p><p>One of the most visible elements of the project will be a new roundabout at the intersection of Eksporta and Čuguna streets. Historic slag carts will be placed at its centre to honour the area’s industrial heritage and symbolically link the history of Liepājas metalurgs with the site’s new stage of development.</p><p>Sustainable thinking is embedded in the “DNA” of Liepāja Industrial Park, with education, innovation and the circular economy established as guiding principles. The former Liepājas metalurgs site is gradually being reintegrated into the city, opening it to the public and making it attractive to investors.</p><p>Liepāja Industrial Park is being developed by the Liepāja SEZ Authority and Liepāja City Municipality, together with partners Liepājas ūdens SIA and Liepājas enerģija SIA, on the former Liepājas metalurgs site between Brīvības, Meldru, Ezermalas, Parka and Zemnieku streets. It borders Lake Liepāja, a specially protected Natura 2000 area. Under the approved development strategy, the plan is to attract more than EUR 275 million in private investment and create at least 1,500 new jobs.</p><p>Real FDI magazine has highlighted ten of the world’s most promising industrial and logistics investment projects, naming Liepāja Industrial Park among them. In today’s competition for investment, locations that offer not only land but also modern infrastructure, energy security, logistics connections and an ecosystem suitable for advanced manufacturing are more attractive. The featured projects span several countries and development models, from major industrial centres in Germany and Spain to emerging logistics hubs in Iceland, Romania, Latvia, the United Kingdom and Mexico.</p><p> </p><p>For reference:</p><ul><li>In 2018, the Ministry of Economics, the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, FeLM SIA, Liepāja City Council and the Liepāja SEZ Authority signed a memorandum of intent on developing the former Liepājas metalurgs site.</li><li>In 2019, consolidation of property ownership at the former Liepājas metalurgs site began.</li><li>In 2021, the development programme for the former Liepājas metalurgs site was approved, and an interinstitutional agreement was concluded with Liepāja City Council on its implementation.</li><li>In 2022, the local spatial plan for the former Liepājas metalurgs site was approved.</li><li>In 2022, a remediation programme for the former Liepājas metalurgs site was prepared.</li><li>In 2023, the Liepāja Industrial Park (LIP) Development Strategy for 2023–2028 was approved.</li><li>In 2023, several construction designs were prepared for infrastructure development at the site.</li><li>In 2023, a project application for the first street construction works at LIP was prepared and submitted, and the Central Finance and Contracting Agency gave a favourable decision on the allocation of funding.</li><li>In 2023, a lease agreement with Liepājas Steel SIA was signed, valid until 31 December 2025, for the dismantling and removal of the electric steelmaking furnace.</li><li>In autumn 2024, construction began on the first street sections and utilities, as well as electrification of the entire site.</li><li>In 2025, the disposal of existing industrial buildings began.</li><li>At the end of 2025, sections of Inženieru, Eksporta, Dzelzgriezēja and Naglu streets were commissioned and opened to the public, together with utilities (water supply, wastewater, stormwater drainage and district heating networks). The project also delivered 3,865 metres of electricity supply networks and eight new transformers across the large site.</li><li>In 2026, an intersection and road were built between Brīvības, Čuguna and Eksporta streets. Savings in project funding also enabled construction of an additional 289-metre section of Martena Street.</li><li>In September 2026, the project “Development of the Liepāja Industrial Park Territory, Phase 2”, approved by the Central Finance and Contracting Agency, began. It includes dismantling the heavy fuel oil facilities, remediating the area and constructing road sections that connect Eksporta and Čuguna streets into a single street network.</li></ul></div>
